1
00:00:00,370 --> 00:00:02,110
All right, so I'll now go to discuss

2
00:00:02,110 --> 00:00:05,830
the evolution of corporate governance in Nigeria and

3
00:00:05,830 --> 00:00:08,070
the culmination into the Nigerian Code of Corporate

4
00:00:08,070 --> 00:00:08,650
Governance.

5
00:00:09,150 --> 00:00:13,450
So pre-1990, the principal company law statute

6
00:00:13,450 --> 00:00:15,810
in Nigeria was the Companies and Allied Matters

7
00:00:15,810 --> 00:00:19,790
Act 1968, which was modelled after the Companies

8
00:00:19,790 --> 00:00:22,410
Act UK of 1948.

9
00:00:23,390 --> 00:00:27,690
Essentially, the Act contained provisions on companies in

10
00:00:27,690 --> 00:00:29,650
relation to the board of directors and the

11
00:00:29,650 --> 00:00:30,890
members in general meeting.

12
00:00:31,890 --> 00:00:34,590
It soon became apparent that that Act in

13
00:00:34,590 --> 00:00:37,430
itself was not enough to regulate the affairs

14
00:00:37,430 --> 00:00:39,130
and activities of companies.

15
00:00:39,770 --> 00:00:43,710
Between the period of 1990 to 2013, many

16
00:00:43,710 --> 00:00:47,550
issues associated with corporate governance failures started to

17
00:00:47,550 --> 00:00:50,830
emerge, and those matters were essentially outside the

18
00:00:50,830 --> 00:00:54,270
legal arena and had more to do with

19
00:00:54,270 --> 00:00:58,670
ethical, concerns rather than legal.

20
00:01:00,010 --> 00:01:02,750
And accordingly, the SEC Code of Corporate Governance

21
00:01:02,750 --> 00:01:06,270
was promulgated, the SEC is the Securities and

22
00:01:06,270 --> 00:01:10,270
Exchange Commission, promulgated a Code of Corporate Governance

23
00:01:10,270 --> 00:01:12,510
in 2011.

24
00:01:13,750 --> 00:01:16,770
Following that, other sectors also came out with

25
00:01:16,770 --> 00:01:18,830
their own Codes of Corporate Governance, notably the

26
00:01:18,830 --> 00:01:24,510
CBN, NICOM, PENCOM, and the Nigerian Communications Commission.

27
00:01:25,350 --> 00:01:28,110
There was an attempt to harmonise all of

28
00:01:28,110 --> 00:01:32,350
these sectorial codes in 2018 with the promulgation

29
00:01:32,350 --> 00:01:36,070
of the Nigerian Code of Corporate Governance 2018,

30
00:01:36,810 --> 00:01:40,210
which has been subsequently amended and came into

31
00:01:40,210 --> 00:01:42,930
operation in 2019.

32
00:01:44,030 --> 00:01:46,810
With the promulgation of the Nigerian Code of

33
00:01:46,810 --> 00:01:50,550
Corporate Governance, the other codes continue to exist

34
00:01:50,550 --> 00:01:54,330
side by side, but recently, as recently as

35
00:01:54,330 --> 00:01:56,950
two years ago, another attempt was made to

36
00:01:56,950 --> 00:01:59,650
streamline those other codes and reduce them to

37
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:02,210
guidelines, such that the only code that we

38
00:02:02,210 --> 00:02:04,110
now have is the Nigerian Code of Corporate

39
00:02:04,110 --> 00:02:07,430
Governance, which is indeed a code of best

40
00:02:07,430 --> 00:02:07,910
practise.

41
00:02:08,470 --> 00:02:10,230
So all the other sectorial codes have been

42
00:02:10,230 --> 00:02:12,870
reduced to guidelines and where there is a

43
00:02:12,870 --> 00:02:16,490
conflict between the guidelines and the code, the

44
00:02:16,490 --> 00:02:20,110
provision with a stricter requirement is the one

45
00:02:20,110 --> 00:02:21,070
that would apply.

46
00:02:21,510 --> 00:02:23,350
So for instance, if the Nigerian Code of

47
00:02:23,350 --> 00:02:27,730
Corporate Governance says that the tenure of non

48
00:02:27,730 --> 00:02:30,790
-executive directors would be at the discretion of

49
00:02:30,790 --> 00:02:33,550
the board and the CBN guideline says it

50
00:02:33,550 --> 00:02:36,090
would be 12 years, the CBN guideline is

51
00:02:36,090 --> 00:02:38,230
the one that would apply because that is

52
00:02:38,230 --> 00:02:42,710
a stricter provision by a regulator regulating that

53
00:02:42,710 --> 00:02:44,470
particular entity.

54
00:02:46,660 --> 00:02:50,060
So just to briefly speak to CAMA, which

55
00:02:50,060 --> 00:02:52,520
is the Communities and Other Matters Act 2020.

56
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:57,180
It was promulgated in 2020, as I said,

57
00:02:57,700 --> 00:03:01,760
and essentially it came to replace the 30

58
00:03:01,760 --> 00:03:05,260
-year-old legislation that was passed in 1968

59
00:03:05,260 --> 00:03:10,400
and has a few governance provisions, interestingly, for

60
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:11,060
the first time.

61
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:14,340
So for instance, it speaks to having independent

62
00:03:14,340 --> 00:03:16,680
directors of the board or public companies.

63
00:03:16,840 --> 00:03:20,280
It also regulates the number of boards a

64
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:22,420
director of a public company can sit on.

65
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:28,960
Interestingly, in February 2023, the government came out

66
00:03:28,960 --> 00:03:32,180
with the Business Facilitation Act, which slightly amended

67
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:35,300
CAMA and also made a few corporate governance

68
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:36,080
provisions.

69
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:39,920
For more details on this, the resources for

